{"id":"tuido-839e8f","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md","action":"updated"},"created_at":"2026-10-02T13:35:39.05202Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.287 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"tuido-2203d3","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-a00371a3-6664-415b-99a9-f41538ca10eb","text":"**Constraint from tuido-839e8f (persisted notification state):** `Todo::notified_at` is system state, not user state. When undo restores a todo from a snapshot, it must **keep the todo's current `notified_at`** rather than the snapshot's value. Otherwise this happens: edit a todo's text, it goes overdue and notifies, undo the edit, and the restored `notified_at: None` triggers a duplicate notification.\n\nRestoring an older `due_date` is fine. The notification logic compares `notified_at` with `due_date`, so it re-arms correctly by itself."},"created_at":"2026-10-02T13:35:39.076115Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.287 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
