{"id":"tuido-2203d3","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md","action":"created"},"created_at":"2026-10-02T13:40:16.65629Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.287 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"tuido-e97675","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Ctrl+\u003cletter\u003e in normal mode triggers the plain-letter action (e.g. Ctrl+D deletes)","description":"Found while speccing tuido-2203d3 (undo/redo).\n\n`App::handle_normal_key` matches `KeyCode::Char(c)` without checking modifiers. So `Ctrl+D` deletes the selected todo, `Ctrl+X`... no, `Ctrl+S` suspends, `Ctrl+R` opens reschedule, and so on. Users can press these by accident, or out of habit from other tools; `Ctrl+D` and `Ctrl+U` are common terminal and pager shortcuts. Redo in tuido-2203d3 needs `Ctrl+R` to mean something else entirely.\n\n**Fix:** in normal mode, a `Char` key with the CONTROL modifier only triggers explicitly bound shortcuts (after tuido-2203d3, just `Ctrl+R` for redo). All other Ctrl+letter combinations are ignored. Shifted letters (`J`, `K`, `X`) are unaffected, because they arrive as uppercase chars.\n\n**Test:** `Ctrl+D` doesn't delete and `Ctrl+S` doesn't suspend.\n\nFixed as part of tuido-2203d3.","sort_order":"a7","dependencies":[{"source_id":"tuido-e97675","target_id":"tuido-2203d3","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["bug"]},"created_at":"2026-10-02T13:41:12.213211Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.287 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"tuido-e97675","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"Found while speccing tuido-2203d3 (undo/redo).\n\n`App::handle_normal_key` matches `KeyCode::Char(c)` without checking modifiers. So `Ctrl+D` deletes the selected todo, `Ctrl+S` suspends, `Ctrl+R` opens reschedule, and so on. Users can press these by accident, or out of habit from other tools; `Ctrl+D` and `Ctrl+U` are common terminal and pager shortcuts. Redo in tuido-2203d3 needs `Ctrl+R` to mean something else entirely.\n\n**Fix:** in normal mode, a `Char` key with the CONTROL modifier only triggers explicitly bound shortcuts (after tuido-2203d3, just `Ctrl+R` for redo). All other Ctrl+letter combinations are ignored. Shifted letters (`J`, `K`, `X`) are unaffected, because they arrive as uppercase chars.\n\n**Test:** `Ctrl+D` doesn't delete and `Ctrl+S` doesn't suspend.\n\nFixed as part of tuido-2203d3.","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-10-02T13:41:42.980737Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.287 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
